bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:76:| `face-address-secret` `fleet-join-custody.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the face's fleet address secret rests in that face, `face-address-secret-g<sha256 of its PersonaGroup>.bin`; the name's digest is the group digest the floor index holds. Local unless a defined face recovery route includes it. |
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:80:| `guardian-set` `:337-338` | face-hot | ''D:'' each face's guardian/recovery commitments under that face; its intended local row rides its one-face bundle. Guardians keep their own secret shares. |
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:97:| `device-share` `:396-397` | face-cold | ''D:'' face-indexed recovery device share, not a common device share. |
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:101:Rows whose class includes ''LEAD'' block byte movement until their payload and owning principal are proved. Current `face` and `bundle` fields describe filename/export selection only (`vault-carriers.ts:119-165`); they never confer cryptographic separation. The existing S-key rotation sweeps all hot/cold rows (`:64-68,477-510`), so K15 replaces it with per-face and named-S rotation.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:135:| Key | Who holds it | What it seals | Where it rests | What unlock needs | Removal and recovery |
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:136:| ''Face custody key (FCK)'' | One PersonaGroup's admitted, unlocked face session; on browser, only the authorized tab's sealer holds bare key material | That face's custody rows: root, Keyhive/Veil archive, face proof/member and recovery material, face-private roster/book/join/outbox partitions. It does not replace per-bag content keys (`sealed-store.mem:65-72`). | Fresh random key, wrapped only in that face's independent slot/recovery tree; bare only during authorized live use. Never wrapped by S key, a node S key, another face, or a common Keyhive archive. | That face's passphrase, paper route, or measured PRF route; each browser tab asks for its own face unlock. A node face act separately proves its face. | Removing a face revokes its slots/admission and retires its local rows without rotating siblings; old offline bundles and already learned plaintext remain readable to their holders. A one-face recovery route restores only that face's FCK and rows. |
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:138:The per-face slot tree carries wrapped FCK, slot identifiers and nonsecret KDF parameters; it cannot embed a sibling wrapper. Bind/unbind/rotation prove the standing FCK and affect that tree and that face's rows alone. The bundle contains only intended wrapped recovery routes, not a bare FCK or guardian-held secret shares. A passkey/PRF slot stands only after its derivation is measured on target devices (`browser-custody.ts:41-43`). Last browser tab/session close drops bare face keys and returns to F (`stable-founding-road.mem:199,202`); an installed app or worker does not extend authority.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:151:Generalize `writeSealedCarrier` from `(key, tree, carrier)` to an explicit `(row class, owner, key, matching tree/door, bound carrier context)`. The caller proves that the key opens the *same owner and row class* against its standing slot tree; an S writer proves its named node door; F has no sealed-carrier writer. Preserve the existing exclusive act, absent-or-authentically-opened standing row rule, and refusal without replacement (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-288`). Seal a full caller-supplied replacement with the same context, write a sidecar, read it back byte-identical, authenticate/open it, then rename (`:290-304`). A failed old-row open, class mismatch, tree mismatch, stale/ambiguous migration shape or failed readback leaves the occupied carrier byte-for-byte unchanged. A whole archive replacement is not an implicit read-modify-write. No encrypt-only capability can perform this overwrite proof. The matching-key rule also governs delete and rotation; never use A's key or node S to authenticate B's standing row.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:160:A bundle for PersonaGroup X contains X's root/identity, X's Keyhive and Veil archive, X's selected proof/recovery keys and rows, X's guardian set/commitments, intended wrapped FCK recovery route, X's selected private roster/book lines, and X's content-key memory section and eligible history. It is sealed under the bundle key and its own route (`sealed-store.mem:68`; `vault-carriers.ts:123-165,301-342,377-397`). It never contains a bare FCK, another face's slot or row, another face's identifiers or content keys, a node place signer, a node S key, guardian-held secret shares, or local-only vessel seat/outbox state. `bundle: stays` remains local unless an explicit per-face recovery rule supersedes it. Restore X into a fresh vessel without opening Y, and refuse an ambiguous old mixed archive. Restore does not mint authority over a sibling.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:169:# ''Isolation red, every row:'' found A and B with independently random FCKs/trees, occupied D carriers for both, and F plus any named node S values. Unlock A alone. Bypass routers, sessions, UI and store adapters; try direct authenticated open, occupied-row replacement, delete, rotation, backup and restore on *each* B carrier using every A key (FCK, member, identity, content, outbox, recovery), all floor values, and all node S/S key legacy values. Every B operation refuses cryptographically and leaves each B occupied ciphertext byte-for-byte unchanged. Copy A ciphertext into B's slot and swap row name/kind: authentication fails. A's same-row replacement succeeds. Restore A's bundle on a fresh vessel: B identity, key, archive and identifiers are absent; B stays unopened. Remove A: B and F still work. This red is stronger than owner-route refusal (`stable-founding-road.mem:459`; `sealed-writer.ts:279-304`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:191:''K15.0, this gate:'' freeze the exhaustive node/browser row census, prove each split and owner selector, define FCK/tree/recovery and carrier context, specify old-shape refusal and the isolation red. No identity or custody bytes move. ''K15.1:'' use the existing two-hive same-process isolation probe, then give each face its own store, registry, archive and key seat. ''K15.2:'' make the face the sole identity/group owner; capture two-face event bytes and reject any shared creator/issuer. ''K15.3:'' route sessions and registries by exactly one face and land ''F6 slice three'' asker sealing with the correct row-class key and occupied-row proof. ''K15.4:'' separate content-key rolls and removal by face, including K12 fresh group behavior. ''K15.5:'' one-face restore and a herm that carries without face authoring. ''K15.6:'' one migration fence for identity bytes, split stores, FCK slot trees, archives and custody formats; refuse old mixed archives by name. This follows `stable-founding-road.mem:459,507`. ''B1'' consumes browser no-S key, per-tab face asks, slot-before-first-secret and last-tab floor (`:199-202,206-207`). F6 routing does not precede the key boundary.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:207:The reds: the index of two founded faces carries no field beyond the entries' tree filename, group digest and ask digest and no byte of either face's name, handle, KEL prefix, root key or label, and reads the same whether the faces are locked or open; swapping two entries' trees makes an unlock open a tree and then refuse at the anchors check, with no face seated. The controls: the digests in the index are the digests the carriers on disk bear, the honest index seats its face, and `vault status` prints the count and the digests alone.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:245:''The serving tree'' (`sk-slots.bin`) stands on its own: the serving key (SK) wraps under its own slots, bound over the founding route with a wrapped key, salt and slot ids of their own, and no other key wraps it. A rotation of the S key moves no byte of it.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:247:''The face tree'' (`faces/<fcid>/fk-slots.bin`) wraps that face's FCK under the face's routes. The three trees stay independent whatever route they share: a rotation or unbind on one leaves the others opening (`face-s-independence.test.ts`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:262:# ''Node private place signing seed'' (ruled 2026-10-19: a scoped online signer under a pre-rotated place KEL whose next key rides the founding card, built in the founding bake; the options it settled follow). Current `.vessel-key*.json` contains a private signing seed in a floor file and `loadVesselSigningSeed` reads it without a human route (`vault-carriers.ts:240-242`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442`). ''Option A:'' keep a *named floor signer* owned by the node, protected at rest under an explicit mechanism; floor means no custody S key/FCK, while this narrowly scoped signer can support faceless Herm startup. Specify the protection, allowed signing/derivation domains, and restart behavior. ''Option B:'' put that signer behind an explicit boot custody door; public card/relay can stand before unlock, while operations requiring its signature wait/refuse until the door opens. Specify Herm boot and unattended container behavior. Neither option is selected here. The private seed is never F, never the current S key, and never the empty serving SK.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:85:* ''Early alpha'': no back-compat, no shims, no legacy recovery; a stale thing aligns to the current frame and grammar, and a third human's arrival ends that line (#/rulings, the fence) (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/wild-bytes">>). Derive, never enumerate. One function, one relation.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:119:* ''Add-a-face (A).'' The daemon writes the public half; the terminal seals the recovery share; seating stays a separate act.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:135:* ''Renewal runs as three sequences, kept apart'' (the realm feed, a per-renewal causal position, the KEL), and lapse arrives as three distinct events: signed departure, recorded removal by relation-scoped kapae, renewal supersession.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:144:* ''The floor key keeps the vessel's public name''; the sealed custody root derives the veil and the sovereign caps. The veil change stays destroy-class.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:145:* ''Unbind offers an S-key rotation loudly'', every time, never folded in silently.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:147:* ''HOT ⊥ COLD'': S key and keyhive state stay hot; the root, the recovery shares and the next-root seed unwrap per act.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:183:* ''The backup bundle'': a bundle holds ONE face, sealed under a route the person holds, and carries that face's root with its own lines and no key of any seat. Its memory rides as content keys, sealed inside the key island: a bundle opens every surviving value ever sealed under a version it carries, values written after its cut included, and no version minted later. A restore runs as a recovery act that takes a fresh veil and a new seat, and one face on a new device reads as the same audience for the versions that face already held (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody">>).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:188:* ''Gate keys come in three tiers'': a floor route's keys derive from the floor key, and a sovereign route's from the sealed custody root (operator ruling); the serving route's bundle derives from SK, a third tier that leaves those two untouched (#/rulings, the ember's serving gate). A faceless place takes a fourth arm at the gate, the place arm, and a session opens before its certificate. The floor key rests outside the vessel key, and the face proof names what a holder of the disk without the passphrase reaches and never reaches (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:189:* ''The widget render boundary stands withdrawn'', and the parser carries recovery (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/graceful-parsing">>).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:206:* ''A face's custody rows seal under its own key'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Each PersonaGroup holds an independent random face custody key (FCK) that only its own slot tree opens (passphrase, paper or PRF), never wrapped under the vessel key, so a holder of the S key or of another face's unlock opens none of its rows. Face-private rows: each face's veil archive, face-key custody, guardian sets, face-provisional, the fleet-join address secret and record, a pending join bound to its target face, the per-face device shares, and each face's partition of the handle book and roster; the vessel Keyhive archive splits per PersonaGroup under `K15`. A face bundle carries that face's identity, archive, FCK recovery route and guardian set, and nothing of a sibling. Today every hot and cold custody row seals under one S key (`vault-carriers.ts`, `browser-custody.ts`), so face isolation reads as routing alone until this lands. Row `K15` carries the build, and `F6` slice three lands with its step 3. How a vessel-common act unlocks dissolves (next ruling).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:207:* ''A shared act never makes a shared secret'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19; four ratcheted research rounds, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026">>). Every act that touches more than one face sorts one of three ways: its private bytes DISSOLVE into the owning face's rows (D), its public bytes stand at the FLOOR by design (F), or it names a vessel SECRET (S) whose principal and opener the house names row by row. A mixed carrier splits before it sorts. The browser holds no vessel key: founding, fleet join, slot changes, roster, archives, handle book, dyads, restore and clear sort D or F, so each tab asks only its own face's unlock and public floor work asks nothing. Phones, installed web apps, probes, the CLI, MCP and watcher seats and a native shell hold no S. A tablet waking floor carries ciphertext and public rows unattended and decrypts no face's row. A container restarts at its floor and nothing unlocks it unattended. A guest island that owns a key store stands as its own vessel. A node holds S only for rows it names: the serving key has no row yet, so it guards nothing today. * ''The place signs by a scoped signer under a pre-rotated KEL'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19; a fifth research round, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026">> #/the-place-signer). A node's place identity keeps a KEL whose inception commits the next key, and that next key rides the founding card beside the seed charter's cold next key, offline; the online place signer runs unattended for an enumerated allowlist of floor duties alone (the KEL's own events excepted, which the card's key signs), rests under the host's TPM-bound credential where the host offers one and an owner-only file elsewhere, and never rides an environment, an image or a log. A stolen online signer rotates away by the card; a lost card beside a stolen signer re-founds. The floor holds no custody key; it holds its own named place signer. The format change rides the founding bake (`X0`). Today the place KEL commits no next key and the seed reaches past the floor (`vessel-kel.ts`; `node-vessel-identity.ts`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:208:* ''Custody and identity read by capability, never by host'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19: //"I consider node and browser isomorphic-by-intent. Eventually, a browser-vessel somewhere may grow comparable capabilities to a node vessel, and this pattern cascades outwards to all future vessels."//). Three rules hold on every vessel, present and planned: a vessel meets each face through that face's atom alone, its veil signing every dial, join, enrolment, sibling channel and daemon proof made on that face's behalf, never one device-wide key; a vessel holds a place signer exactly while it stands a floor, for the floor's allowlisted duties, under the pre-rotated place KEL; and a faceless vessel reading another's floor proves nothing private, reading the public shelf under the floor card's pin. A node's daemon auth, device-key signer, sibling enrolment and group-secret opener read the one vessel seed today (`open-node-vessel.ts`), and a browser's do the same (`open-browser-vessel.ts`); each is a bond owed, carried by `K15`.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:218:* ''The ember's key at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A separate serving key (SK), the fifth custody class `serving`, wrapped by the vessel key and by ember slots, seals the serving rows alone, and the serving tier holds no minting secret: a walker's grant checks under a public half, a shape the hosted road's re-cut lands before the ember's walker arm. An unattended answer (any slot that answers without a person's confirm on the ember) needs a freshness anchor a disk copy cannot carry, a hardware-sealed hearth key whose attestation the ember checks on each ask; without one every ember slot answers only on a person's confirm, threshold two buys a second confirm and never an unattended raise, and after any counted removal a bare deny-only `sk-rotation-owed` mark refuses every ask until SK rolls, a mark a rolled-back disk copy can omit. ''For the operator'': a hearth that holds no hardware anchor raises only on a person's confirm. The reason: a key that opens serving state opens nothing beside it, and a copied disk replays any freshness it carries; the ruling flips on a threat review showing the serving rows hold nothing a thief can use even before the grant re-cut (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember">>).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:225:* ''The restore's key history'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A restored face stands PROVISIONAL until at least one party to its relation (a sibling device of the same human, a seated chair of its Nexus, or a guardian) shows its own log view and that view agrees with the restore's head; while provisional the face reads, recovers and serves its own content and signs no authority-changing act, the restore prints which head it read and from where, and the provisional state names who could confirm it. Where every such party stays unreachable, the guardians' recovery path (the impersonation quorum) answers, never the local read alone. The reason: no vessel reads a head it never received, and the house keeps no registry of heads; no flip stands, and the residue stays stated: one agreeing party may itself sit stale (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:230:* ''Engine cadence'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Cadence keeps its own lineage and threshold, apart from every seat key. A Nexus's joint founding cites one cadence inception that names each founder's hearth, the device that bears the reboot, as a holder; a household seed carries none, since its one holder bears its reboots. Each hearth mints its cadence key at random on the device, never derived from serving or floor material, and keeps it, with its next-key commitment, as a `cold` row under the vessel key that unwraps per act; no sixth custody class and no extra card at the founding. A holder may later rotate onto a cold card by its own choice. Each island pins one engine epoch, and one daemon hosts two epochs only as two separately pinned islands. The reason: the people who bear a reboot decide it, and a seat-holder who runs no hardware forces no one's restart. The ruling flips on a cadence act that must run unattended where a per-act unwrap cannot (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/contract">>).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:232:* ''A restore after guardian enrolment'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A face's own bundle restores after it enrols guardians, so long as the face's log still holds the set its own root enrolled by the typed first event and no later event moved it; a guardian rotation that moves the set cuts every earlier bundle off. The restored face stands provisional as the restore's key-history ruling holds. The reason: enrolling guardians must never silently kill a person's own backups, and the guardians' rotation, not the enrolment, cuts off a stolen bundle. The ruling flips on a provisional gate that cannot stop a restored face from signing an authority-changing act before a party confirms it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:233:* ''Paper restores a root, never memory'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). The split-root cards rebuild the persona root alone; content keys stay random and wrap under the vessel key or a bundle's own key, and no key the root derives opens memory. A person recovers memory through a bundle, under the route that bundle seals. The reason: a root card that opened memory would turn every share holder and every thief of the paper into a reader of the face's private bytes. The ruling flips on an explicit choice that root-share holders read memory, with the custody that choice costs stated.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:236:* ''The working address'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A face's working document takes its id from a dedicated face address secret: 32 random bytes minted once per face, sealed on each of that face's vessels, delivered to a joining vessel by the fleet-join door, and moved only by a deliberate rotation act that opens a fresh working document; neither a per-Nexus key's re-issue nor a vessel key touches it. The id reads as the first 16 bytes of HMAC-SHA-256 keyed by that secret over the length-prefixed domain tag `lar/working-address/v1`, the wiki relation's stable id (the face's `wikiId`, never a recipe CID, a composition fingerprint or a label, so a recipe edit or a rename moves nothing), and the slot kind in its lowercase spelling (`draft`, `working` or `personal`; `temp` holds no document). Each working document starts empty, with no initial change, so two offline vessels of one face converge on one history; a vessel's first write lands as an ordinary change. The reason: two devices of one face open one notebook offline and sync into one, while a keyed id lets no observer link two faces or read the secret. The ruling flips on a face identity contract from train N that already names shared face-secret bytes with the same custody and rotation.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:251:* ''Sync carries; verification decides'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the research at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/identity-invariants-2026">> and the controller's leans). A mutable CRDT carries identity claims and never decides them. Each face holds, on each device, a ''sealed identity anchor'' in its own custody (a D row): its PersonaGroup, its persona-KEL prefix, a commitment to its root, its device edge's digest, and a known frontier that never falls. Only founding, admission and the recovery rites (guardian rotation, bundle restore, root cards) write an anchor; sync never writes one. Founding and admission acts, device edges and lease advances travel as signed records in the face's `@persona` plane, a signed-entry log every reader verifies against the anchor and the KEL; two signed acts at one sequence surface as a fork, never a CRDT winner. The face planes hold verified projections. A device reads freely; an act that widens authority (admit, grant, re-key, seal to a reader) checks the sealed frontier and refuses a target that names a newer bound, its receipt naming the frontier it acted under. Transparency and witnesses for equivocation wait for their own row. Wave C carries the build inside `K15` step 6.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:256:* ''The edge frontier'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the controller's leans after the research at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/edge-frontier-2026">>). Nothing signed today lets a reader tell a device never set aside from a raise erased or withheld, so the house adds a signed ''edge frontier'' per board, its own domain-separated certificate type, the persona KEL resolving its signer's key. Every hand that can raise on a board signs its own stream's frontier (a fleet board's root; a realm board's charter quorum, with its holder where vouch edges raise), and a frontier commits its counted acts through its boundary as a content-addressed snapshot, its parent frontier, and its roster and epoch lineage. A raise shadows its target at once for every reader that sees it, and counts toward widening once its signer registers it into the next frontier. Sync always carries acts, frontiers and snapshots. A widening act (admit, grant, a content key sealed or re-sealed to a reader) reads its target against a frontier of every raising hand that extends any head the reader pinned and includes every raise it has seen; it acts as of that frontier, its receipt naming it, and waits as unknown where none qualifies. Every fleet and realm founds with a signed empty genesis frontier, and an admit bundle carries the current frontier and its snapshot. Until the row stands built, absence reads clear for sync and for widening alike, the gap named. Row `K17`.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:336:* ''The device recovery share'': each face's device share rests as one COLD row under the S key, opened for the act that mints it and the quorum that assembles it (`packages/lararium-node/src/recovery-share-store.ts`). Nothing at a face's path reads as none landed; a row that stands and does not open, or opens to another shape, refuses the read and the write by name, so a wrong key replaces no share and no split lands over one it could not read. A share lands once per generation: a second split of the same generation refuses, since it would orphan the off-device shares a person already holds. A split hands the person both off-device shares before the device share rests, so a run that stops between the two leaves nothing sealed and the same command splits afresh.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:341:* ''The gate's two root doors'' (specified in code; no composer calls them until the socket cutover): a floor gate's bundle derives through the floor key's door, which answers at a locked vessel, hands back a fresh child and never the floor key (`vesselFloorKeyDoor`, `packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts`); a sovereign gate's bundle derives through the custody seat's door, which answers only while the seat holds its key (`custodyGateDoor`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-seat.ts`). The doors carry two types, so no line swaps them by accident; a root refuses the other tier's door by name; each seat's door refuses the other tier's child; and a weld holds the two mints to the two seats (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:343:* ''The session and the boot'': the daemon stands at the floor holding no S key and unlocks on a custody frame of its owner-only socket (`unlock` · `lock` · `custody` · `act` · `backup`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-frames.ts`), handled ahead of every verb, so the credential the CLI reads on its TTY reaches no receipt, outcome or log. One holder keeps the opened S key in node-main (`custody-holder.ts`); an unlock tears the standing composition down and stands a fresh one on the same floor (`open-node-vessel.ts`), a lock returns it to the floor, and three failed unlocks spend a boot's attempts. A composition that refuses above the floor leaves the floor standing and names its fault once. The settle sweeps a sidecar whose write never landed. The test door (`tools/unlock-test-custody.mjs`) opens marked scratch roots alone, and the marker earns one loud line in `vault status` and the boot log. Every boot composes at the floor: nothing stored lifts it. A daemon island holds no S key, so its archive exports cross the custody write door (`custody:write`) and the main thread seals them. Every broad sync port, the daemon island's and each wiki island's, crosses one hardened facade on both ends: an arrival that reads as no sync message drops at the port and never reaches a listener that would throw on it (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/island-repo.ts`). The daemon island's gates ask the main thread what a device's edge reads on its fleet's board through the dyad door (`dyad:ask`), which answers `aside` wherever it cannot read. Each fold of the fleet's edge board sets off the key side's settle on an unlocked vessel, with no timer and no person: the main thread places the island's `seat-duties` verb, awaits its answer and keeps the signed rolls (`packages/lararium-node/src/seat-settle.ts`; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/what-the-code-holds">>). Those two doors and the rest door answer the daemon island's channel alone: each worker's class binds at its spawn (`packages/lararium-node/src/worker-handle.ts`), a wiki island reaches the fetch door alone while its custody write and its rest ask refuse by name and its dyad ask draws `aside`, and a session island reaches no vessel door. A command that acts with no vessel standing opens its own key session at the first sealed row it touches (`packages/lares-cli/src/offline-custody.ts`): a terminal prompt, and under a marked scratch root the published test route. A sealed row's act rides an `act` frame of the custody door and never a store verb: one typed table bounds the acts (`packages/lararium-node/src/sealed-acts.ts`), a standing vessel answers with no second prompt, and beside a standing vessel a command opens no key session of its own, while a root's recovery split refuses there (`packages/lares-cli/src/sealed-act-door.ts`). The fleet mirror of a pet-name and a declared Handle rides that door too: `selves.carry` names a face and which name, the vessel's main thread reads the name off its sealed row and writes it onto that face's own persona plane and no other face's, only where that plane's rest seals (a clear rest refuses the carry and the name stays on its row), `lares persona sync` fills and never replaces, and no verb takes or answers a face's own name (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape">>).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:345:* ''The per-face backup bundle, its restore'': `lares vault restore <file>` brings a face back onto a fresh vessel as a recovery act, under the law at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>. The vessel checks the root against the newest key history it reads, seals one commit row, lands the carried rows additively, and lights the face through the face rite: a new seat under the same fixed prefix (`packages/lararium-node/src/face-bundle-restore.ts`; the act table's `bundle.restore` and `bundle.import` rows; `packages/lares-cli/src/vault-restore.ts`). The rehearsal runs end to end on scratch roots, on the real doors and on standing vessels of the built tree. `--memory` lands the bundle's content keys through the island's import door; on a production vessel it refuses `signer-unavailable` until the wire cutover opens a session signer.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:346:* ''The custody door and its census'': `lares vault` answers seven verbs (`status` · `unlock` · `lock` · `bind` · `unbind` · `backup` · `restore`), and a credential rides a terminal alone: no flag, environment variable or pipe carries one. `vault status` gives ONE reading of each standing file, derived from the carrier table: a `hot` or `cold` row reads sealed or reads as a fault, a row outside the S key prints the law it rests bare under or reads as a fault where the file breaks that law, and a private file in the three homes that no row names prints by name (`vault-carriers.ts` `outsideTheTable`; `custody-session.ts` `custodyStatus`). `vault unbind --rotate` runs as one act: the rotation's census and its opening of every carrier pass before any byte moves, so a carrier that refuses the rotation leaves the slot bound. `tools/custody-census-witness.sh` founds a vessel on a marked scratch root with the built tree and refuses an owner-only file, a seal or a sidecar outside the table; it carries that rotation to its end on the founded vessel. The law, each bare row's reason and the residue a row's name leaves at rest read at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:363:''R-CHARTER, the one AID train.'' Its first stage stands landed (train K of #/the-trains): the chairs sign with seat keys of their own and pre-rotate on cold cards, a Nexus answers to `nexus-<sha>`, the floor key commits no next key, per-Nexus keys mint at random, the PersonaGroup stands as a Keyhive group, and the Stable founding act founds a hearth (`lares vessel found --hearth`): the place, persona zero and a PRIVATE seed charter at one seat, with no keyring, every fleet relation bound to that charter's AID, and every harness that founds a hearth founding through it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/found">>). What stays owed:
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:369:* A per-Nexus wire key in two tiers (operator ruling): a floor route's bundle derives from the floor key and answers at a locked vessel; a sovereign route's bundle derives from the sealed custody root and stands only while the vessel stands unlocked. One gate per (Nexus, route), so knock, pin, verdict and posture read per Nexus exactly (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>). The serving route's third tier rides train E (`E3`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:406:* ''Mutual removal'' (operator ruling; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae">>): both signed acts and both key branches stay, every member sees the conflict, and it ends in an explicit fork unless the realm's charter named a recovery council.
