<<~ ahu #/the-floor-index>>

!!! The floor index of faces (ruled 2026-10-23)

A node with no omnibus key still has to name the faces it can unlock before any face opens, so the identity home keeps ONE F row, `face-index`, in the clear. It lists the node's faces in local face order. Each entry holds the SHA-256 digest of its PersonaGroup (null while the founding stands), the digest of the founding's ask (null once the group stands) and the filename of the face's own slot tree. It holds no name, no handle, no prefix, no label and no key bytes.

''The accepted leak, and only it:'' the count of the node's faces, tombstones included (so a removal history leaks as a count); each PersonaGroup's digest; each founding ask's digest; and each tree's random filename. Nothing else: no name, no handle, no prefix, no label, no key byte, and nothing derived from the face custody id beyond the filename itself. The digests already name the carriers on disk (`face-atom-g<digest>.bin`, `veil-archive-g<digest>.bin`), so the index shows a stolen disk no fact it does not already show. A removed face leaves a tombstone entry, so no other face's local ordinal moves.

''The index routes and never decides.'' It rests at F, unauthenticated, so a swapped or tampered entry could point a group at another face's tree. After an unlock, the D anchors row under that face's own FCK names its PersonaGroup; the vessel compares that group with the index entry's digest and refuses by name on a mismatch, before it seats anything. The index never chooses a worn face's authority either: `readFace` reads the sealed anchor.

''The owner in every D context is the face custody id (`fcid`),'' a random 16-byte id minted with the face's slot tree at the founding's start. It is immutable and never reused. Rows that exist before the PersonaGroup does (the persona root, the founding's atom ask) seal under it, and so do the rows after, so nothing re-seals when the group arrives. The anchors row binds the fcid to the PersonaGroup. Rows are local and never cross vessels, so the id needs no cross-vessel stability.

The reds: the index of two founded faces carries no field beyond the entries' tree filename, group digest and ask digest and no byte of either face's name, handle, KEL prefix, root key or label, and reads the same whether the faces are locked or open; swapping two entries' trees makes an unlock open a tree and then refuse at the anchors check, with no face seated. The controls: the digests in the index are the digests the carriers on disk bear, the honest index seats its face, and `vault status` prints the count and the digests alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/custody-derive-audit>>

!! The custody root and the Keyhive archive: the owner audit

Wave D audit of every child the sealed custody root derives and of the vessel's Keyhive archive, read off the code (slice 2, ruling 7).

; The custody root's children
: ''One child derives today.'' The only call that reaches `holder.derive` is `custodyGateDoor` (`custody-seat.ts`), and its only consumers are `hearthSovereignPin`, `stoodSovereignPin` and the gate composition in `open-node-vessel.ts`: each derives the SOVEREIGN GATE BUNDLE (`GATE_BUNDLE_INFO`) of one Nexus and route. The registry (`ALL_DOMAINS`) declares other domains, and the door derives under any of them, but no other caller derives. The veil, Keyhive signer and hosting key named in the root's header derive from the vessel key, a face's atom or their own rows, never from this root.
: ''Owner: the node, not a face.'' The bundle keys the hearth's gate at a Nexus: the node's seat in that Nexus, apart from any PersonaGroup. It is a NODE-ONLY service secret and enters S as the separately named row `gate-root` (the custody root renamed for what it derives). It is never retained as a generic root, and no face derivation hangs from it.
: ''Stands:'' `gate-root` rests under the node's S key (the node route), and a derivation under any other domain refuses by name at the door.
* ''Full models land early'': #/the-attractor.
* ''One roadmap'': this carrier. Standing procedures rest at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures">>, and the grammar watch at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/grammar-watch">>.
* ''The sealed store'': cleartext lives in the live wiki and the operator's projected files alone. Private bytes take randomized authenticated sealing addressed over the ciphertext; the private tier takes no convergent sealing, and no opener returns unauthenticated bytes. A content key carries a version and rolls at a causal removal act. History at admission must have stood declared when written, and one logical bag name carries its key-roll epochs (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store">>).
* ''The backup bundle'': a bundle holds ONE face, sealed under a route the person holds, and carries that face's root with its own lines and no key of any seat. Its memory rides as content keys, sealed inside the key island: a bundle opens every surviving value ever sealed under a version it carries, values written after its cut included, and no version minted later. A restore runs as a recovery act that takes a fresh veil and a new seat, and one face on a new device reads as the same audience for the versions that face already held (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody">>).
* ''Removal's reach'': four edges take a marker (dyad, vouch, member, carriage). A vouch edge answers to its voucher and to a chartered quorum; a seal roll carries every shadow; a standing shadow reaches every gate and drives a key roll; a contested tie keeps the shadow up; mutual removal ends in an explicit fork (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/four-edges">>).
* ''The PersonaGroup stands as a Keyhive group'', so a seat's revocation rolls the key of every bag the group reaches (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/keys">>; a fresh group stays owed, `K12` of #/the-loops).
* ''The hands'': every hand opens a session. A tasked spirit comes down to an AI, a Mask and the vessel's seat, attended or standing; the vessel's own key serves as a spirit's system identity; a Mask counts as no pet-name. The door carries and the hand authorizes, over one verb table, and a standing seat never signs an act that changes who holds authority (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands">>).
* ''Sealed-row acts ride the custody door'', never a store verb whose output lands in the daemon's document in the clear (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape">>).
* ''Gate keys come in three tiers'': a floor route's keys derive from the floor key, and a sovereign route's from the sealed custody root (operator ruling); the serving route's bundle derives from SK, a third tier that leaves those two untouched (#/rulings, the ember's serving gate). A faceless place takes a fourth arm at the gate, the place arm, and a session opens before its certificate. The floor key rests outside the vessel key, and the face proof names what a holder of the disk without the passphrase reaches and never reaches (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>).
* ''The widget render boundary stands withdrawn'', and the parser carries recovery (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/graceful-parsing">>).

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-10-16 ~ approved as the controller's story leans
* ''A joined device reads the hearth's corpus and keeps its own.'' A joinee mounts the hearth's `@lararium` corpus read-only beside its own, and its writes stay in its own; the oracle plane (the hearth's catalog, daemon doc and faces' planes) never crosses (`device-admit.ts#admitCanonDocs`). Row `W21` carries the build.
* ''The bag plane hides its creator before strangers carry it.'' The carried bag event's creator identifier in the clear gets measured red-first and ranked before wave three: ahead of anything that lets a third human's data cross a herm. Row `W25`.
* ''An agent's dissent drafts; a person's ask edits'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract">> #/the-hands): an agent never overrides a person's edit, and a person's direct ask to edit a tiddler makes the edit the person's own act, bounded by the ask and the asker's authority.
* ''The system wikis release; every other wiki reads the floor'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-16). Only each Nexus's Kahu cabal writes the `lares` and `lararium` wikis and bags live. Every other wiki, and every other hand, mounts them read-only by CID: the latest release that cabal approved. The release rides the Nexus's crossroads board as its public answer, naming the floor CID under the Kahu steward's seal. The cabal approves the exact bytes under its charter's threshold, and a herm carries the answer without signing it. Every wiki mounts that floor through `@oracle`, which never writes. A vessel moves its floor pin only when it accepts a newer signed release. With no Nexus speaking, it stands on its last accepted floor, or on genesis. A face's wikis follow the floor of the Nexus that face stands in, so one vessel may hold two floors and never ties its faces together. An edit aimed at the floor copies up into the wiki's own working layer, and an offering back travels as a draft into the cabal's realm. A lone hearth is a Nexus of one, so its operator stands as its own Kahu. Row `W26` carries the build.
* DreamDOM's ruled questions stand in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom">> #/open-rulings.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-10-18
* ''Each browser tab asks for its own unlock.'' A tab that attaches to the shared holder opens the keystore by its own ask of the person; the holder carries no unlock between tabs. The reason reads off the session law: a session wears its own face, so each tab answers for its own opening (#/rulings, a session wears the face). The ask admits a tab to its face's open hive and derives no key: the holder keeps a face's keys while some session of that face stands unlocked, and the last to close locks it (operator, 2026-10-18; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026">> #/what-both-faces-hold). Row `B1` carries the build.
* ''A face's secrets never rest clear, even at a first join'' (operator, 2026-10-18, approving the house lean). A fresh device's fleet join binds a passphrase or paper slot before the join lands, since every face secret refuses to rest outside the slot tree. Row `B1`.
* ''On the node, the boot marks the unlock and the session marks the face'' (operator, 2026-10-18, approving the house reading). The per-tab ask binds browser tabs; a node unlocks once per boot through its custody door, and every CLI run, MCP seat and watcher proves its own face at the gate without asking again (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026">> #/the-node-scope).
* ''The last tab closes, and the browser rests at its floor'' (operator, 2026-10-18, approving the house lean). The unlock lives in page memory alone, so the browser vessel's sovereign composition ends with its last tab and the next tab asks again. Row `B1`.
* ''One Keyhive identity per PersonaGroup, none above them'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18: //"it's the only way to retain control over faces that don't know about each other"//). Each face's PersonaGroup owns its Keyhive identity, its event store, its registry, its archive and its content-key seat; no vessel-wide identity registers a face's bags, delegates them, or holds a face's group. Two faces of one vessel then never link on the wire, a removal from one face rolls that face's bags alone, and a bundle restores one face and nothing of another. Today the vessel's own identity registers every bag and delegates each face's bags through itself, and the vessel and every face share one event store and one content-key holder (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/keyhive-per-persona-group-2026">>). House readings under the operator's confidence, each naming what flips it: a herm keeps a floor-only transport key, behind a red test that it can neither author, resolve nor observe a face's capabilities, and holds no Keyhive identity if that test fails; several face hives share one process only after a substrate probe reads them isolated, and each face's hive runs in its own worker otherwise; co-mingled history migrates by fresh identities and an old mixed archive refuses by name, with no bridge; a founder that cannot leave its group leaves by a fresh PersonaGroup. Row `K15` carries the build, folding `W25` and `K12`; `W26`'s slice 3 waits on its face context.
* ''A cabal shares through the faces its members choose'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18, on the Teshima Family Council story). Kalani founds a private family council; Leilani, already standing on the DreamNet with several faces, chooses which of them join it, and a face she keeps back never learns the council exists. The council's hive reads as a Keyhive group with no key of its own: its members act through their own faces' identities, so nothing stands above them, and a presentation reads valid against the current set without naming which member made it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/identity-classes">> #/the-handle-chain). Any current member's face promotes into the council's canon; a graft takes the threshold; a face leaves by its own act, and another's removal rides `kapae` at the threshold or a fork that omits the captor (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm">> #/the-unswept-corner); members read the owner-set as faces, never as humans. A private council declares no heraldry and lives in its members' address books under pet-names (cabal-realm #/heraldry). Row `K16` carries the story as `K15`'s acceptance.
* ''The asker seals'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19, F6's custody fork). Once the browser holder owns the Repo and the daemon kernel, it routes each sealed write to the admitted tab whose session caused it, else to any admitted, unlocked tab of that write's face; with no awake admitted tab, the write waits, named `seal-awaits-session`, never silent and never decided by a clock. No key material leaves a tab, and each sealer holds the row-class key the occupied carrier's overwrite proof opens. A SharedWorker carries no promise to run while its tabs freeze, and no `MessagePort` event reports a peer's death (round one, 2026-10-19), so a seal that waits names its wait.
* ''A face's custody rows seal under its own key'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Each PersonaGroup holds an independent random face custody key (FCK) that only its own slot tree opens (passphrase, paper or PRF), never wrapped under the vessel key, so a holder of the S key or of another face's unlock opens none of its rows. Face-private rows: each face's veil archive, face-key custody, guardian sets, face-provisional, the fleet-join address secret and record, a pending join bound to its target face, the per-face device shares, and each face's partition of the handle book and roster; the vessel Keyhive archive splits per PersonaGroup under `K15`. A face bundle carries that face's identity, archive, FCK recovery route and guardian set, and nothing of a sibling. Today every hot and cold custody row seals under one S key (`vault-carriers.ts`, `browser-custody.ts`), so face isolation reads as routing alone until this lands. Row `K15` carries the build, and `F6` slice three lands with its step 3. How a vessel-common act unlocks dissolves (next ruling).
* ''A shared act never makes a shared secret'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19; four ratcheted research rounds, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026">>). Every act that touches more than one face sorts one of three ways: its private bytes DISSOLVE into the owning face's rows (D), its public bytes stand at the FLOOR by design (F), or it names a vessel SECRET (S) whose principal and opener the house names row by row. A mixed carrier splits before it sorts. The browser holds no vessel key: founding, fleet join, slot changes, roster, archives, handle book, dyads, restore and clear sort D or F, so each tab asks only its own face's unlock and public floor work asks nothing. Phones, installed web apps, probes, the CLI, MCP and watcher seats and a native shell hold no S. A tablet waking floor carries ciphertext and public rows unattended and decrypts no face's row. A container restarts at its floor and nothing unlocks it unattended. A guest island that owns a key store stands as its own vessel. A node holds S only for rows it names: the serving key has no row yet, so it guards nothing today. * ''The place signs by a scoped signer under a pre-rotated KEL'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19; a fifth research round, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026">> #/the-place-signer). A node's place identity keeps a KEL whose inception commits the next key, and that next key rides the founding card beside the seed charter's cold next key, offline; the online place signer runs unattended for an enumerated allowlist of floor duties alone (the KEL's own events excepted, which the card's key signs), rests under the host's TPM-bound credential where the host offers one and an owner-only file elsewhere, and never rides an environment, an image or a log. A stolen online signer rotates away by the card; a lost card beside a stolen signer re-founds. The floor holds no custody key; it holds its own named place signer. The format change rides the founding bake (`X0`). Today the place KEL commits no next key and the seed reaches past the floor (`vessel-kel.ts`; `node-vessel-identity.ts`).
* ''Custody and identity read by capability, never by host'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19: //"I consider node and browser isomorphic-by-intent. Eventually, a browser-vessel somewhere may grow comparable capabilities to a node vessel, and this pattern cascades outwards to all future vessels."//). Three rules hold on every vessel, present and planned: a vessel meets each face through that face's atom alone, its veil signing every dial, join, enrolment, sibling channel and daemon proof made on that face's behalf, never one device-wide key; a vessel holds a place signer exactly while it stands a floor, for the floor's allowlisted duties, under the pre-rotated place KEL; and a faceless vessel reading another's floor proves nothing private, reading the public shelf under the floor card's pin. A node's daemon auth, device-key signer, sibling enrolment and group-secret opener read the one vessel seed today (`open-node-vessel.ts`), and a browser's do the same (`open-browser-vessel.ts`); each is a bond owed, carried by `K15`.
* ''Identity enacts a bond'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Lineage stays within a root, and covenant joins roots: a root (a compartment seed, a vessel key, a face custody key, a charter seat, a content key) derives children for its own duties alone, and any identity spanning two roots is a bond, an act both ends sign that seats a fresh key of its own, resting with the end whose privacy it serves and lapsing by its own act; no closure holds a root (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/identity-enacts-relation">> #/identity-as-bond, its seven laws and bond table).
* ''A face's device veil is its own random key'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19, re-reading the 2026-09-05 veil ruling). Each PersonaGroup's veil on a device mints at random when the device joins that face and rests under that face's FCK, so no device seed stands as an ancestor of any face's veil; it stays vessel-local, never presents, and reads mint-shaped to every observer. The group-secret opener follows it.
* ''Each realm stands under one charter, a seed's or a Nexus's'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18). A council that meets at one hearth stands under that hearth's seed charter, and its guests' chosen faces walk there with no Nexus founded; a council two hearths carry stands under the Nexus those two found fresh, since a Nexus names the operators' relationship (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae">> #/edge-hands). A guest sees every realm its worn face stands in.
* ''A write lands on a working layer, and canon takes it by promotion'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18). A `LOAD` lands on a wiki's working slot alone; canon takes content only by the cap-gated promotion `MOVE` (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-layer-ontology">>), and genesis and founding seed canon through their own named door. An act aimed at a wiki no island mounts asks the vessel to mount it, and refuses gracefully, naming the cause, when the mount cannot stand. Row `W27` carries the build.
* ''Every bag opens as its own wiki'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18). The floor recipe names the bag alone over `@oracle`, derived and never minted, and an absent record reads as that floor, never as "no such wiki"; a composed wiki's record, which a hand authors, lays several bags and plugin offerings beneath the wiki's own canon bag (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-layer-ontology">> #/quine). No act enumerates bags to mint wikis. Row `W27` carries the build.
* ''One owner per registry; the floor reads by CID'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18: //"Architecture stands, leans stand"//). The `@oracle` document as a registry every vessel writes retires: one id carried one `lares` and one `lararium` pointer for the whole fleet, and a joined vessel's pointer replaced its hearth's, a shape a two-vessel probe and three shard runs measured. `@oracle` stands as the floor's read-face over a manifest CID, which a wiki mounts read-only by the pin its face accepted. Each registry carries only what its scope's owner writes: a vessel's bootstrap holds its own document pointers and its accepted floor pins; `@catalog` the operator's documents; the Kahu realm the live `lares` and `lararium` documents and their drafts; the crossroads board the Nexus's one public answer. The leans the ruling adopts: the Kahu realm holds the live system documents, with `bags/` projected on the Kahu's vessel alone; a one-seat founding lands a counted 1-of-1 Kahu-cabal admission for persona zero's group, so one predicate decides every hand's reach into system canon; the release answer, the Nexus's one public answer, names the Nexus AID, the charter epoch, the release before it and the floor manifest CID, sealed by the steward, with the promotion approval over the manifest's exact bytes riding attached; a vessel records an accepted release as a sealed hot row per (face, Nexus) that only a person's act moves; genesis bakes the first floor, a corpus CID in the seed. A signing domain of its own carries the release, `floor-release`, so no seal over another shape verifies as one (operator-approved, 2026-10-18). The shape and its slices stand at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w26-floor-lane">>; rows `W26`, `W21` and `P8` carry the build.

!!! House rulings under the operator's delegation ~ the questions the operator delegated, each with what flips it
The operator delegated these to the house: each stands as a house ruling the operator may overturn, its law lives in the carrier it names, and its build rides the loop rows of #/the-loops.
* ''The ember's key at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A separate serving key (SK), the fifth custody class `serving`, wrapped by the vessel key and by ember slots, seals the serving rows alone, and the serving tier holds no minting secret: a walker's grant checks under a public half, a shape the hosted road's re-cut lands before the ember's walker arm. An unattended answer (any slot that answers without a person's confirm on the ember) needs a freshness anchor a disk copy cannot carry, a hardware-sealed hearth key whose attestation the ember checks on each ask; without one every ember slot answers only on a person's confirm, threshold two buys a second confirm and never an unattended raise, and after any counted removal a bare deny-only `sk-rotation-owed` mark refuses every ask until SK rolls, a mark a rolled-back disk copy can omit. ''For the operator'': a hearth that holds no hardware anchor raises only on a person's confirm. The reason: a key that opens serving state opens nothing beside it, and a copied disk replays any freshness it carries; the ruling flips on a threat review showing the serving rows hold nothing a thief can use even before the grant re-cut (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember">>).
* ''The ember's serving gate'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A third gate tier, the serving route, takes its bundle from SK, and the floor and sovereign tiers stand untouched; a pin moves only by a signed causal event or a verified key-history step, and no quiet period ages a pin out. The reason: a dialer that pinned the sovereign tier never mistakes a serving hearth for an unlocked one. The ruling flips on proof that a gate pin decides no authority anywhere (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>).
* ''How a raise lapses'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A raise lives one boot and lapses at the next causal boundary (a lock, a restart, a human unlock, a counted removal), and that boundary IS the expiry the waking floor requires; no timer and no heartbeat ends a standing, and the count spends at the raise ledger's durable commit before SERVING composes, so a crash before the commit spends nothing and serves nothing and a crash after it burns the raise. The reason: quiet never tells a slow device from a gone one; the residue: a rolled-back ledger on a disk copy restores spent raises. The ruling flips on a causal renewal event every reader receives that also survives the ember's loss (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape">>).
* ''A removal no reader can keep'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). The based removal refuses by name where no parallel-drafts reader keeps the concurrent words, or every reader failed, and the store authors nothing; the refusal carries a durable conflict handle (the document, the removal's request id, the conflicting change and head identities, never the hand's text), so a hand whose editor went offline finds the same conflict on its return. The reason: a draft the store wrote would choose a bag, a tier and a steward no hand named. The ruling flips on an already-authorized destination for such words that keeps provenance and tier without the store choosing either (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/automerge-doc-store">>).
* ''The outbox at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each outbox parcel seals under randomized authenticated encryption with a stable outbox key the vessel key wraps and an unlock hands the daemon island, the way content keys reach it, and rests under a random name with its routing inside the sealed body, so a locked disk names no reader; the `sealed-outbox` row leaves `floor-plain` for a sealed class. Before a parcel's atomic commit nothing stands sendable, after it the parcel re-opens and retries at the next unlock, and after a ship and before the local delete the receiver folds a repeat idempotently. The reason: a reader digest on a stolen disk names a relationship; no flip stands expected, since the ruling closes a residue (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>).
* ''The herm relay leg'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). The ask rides the sealed sibling session under that session's seal with a tagged inner frame, and the delivery receipt stays a bare session note good for the asker alone, bound to a unique request id and the session's causal position, attesting transit and never execution; reach narrows to one PersonaGroup, and the leg lands with `W9`. The reason: the fewest new shapes, and no signing domain minted for a transport fact. The ruling flips on a concrete need for a third party to audit relay delivery after the asker vanishes (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/receipt-ledger#/the-relay-leg">>).
* ''The disk watcher's reach'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A pending removal settles with no person present only under a standing instruction the operator signs by a human route, naming one bag and a bounded class of removals (a path pattern and a per-wave count ceiling): the watcher executes within it, every settled removal lands in the audit with the instruction it rode, and `lares shore restore` still answers it. An automated seat never signs one, a standoff (disk and records differ) never settles without a person, and a mass vanish past the instruction's ceiling stays pending for a person. The reason: a person who answered in writing for exactly that class has answered, and a count alone proves no intent; the ruling flips on a single incident where an in-scope instruction deleted what a person meant to keep (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/projection-law">>).
* ''The restore's key history'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A restored face stands PROVISIONAL until at least one party to its relation (a sibling device of the same human, a seated chair of its Nexus, or a guardian) shows its own log view and that view agrees with the restore's head; while provisional the face reads, recovers and serves its own content and signs no authority-changing act, the restore prints which head it read and from where, and the provisional state names who could confirm it. Where every such party stays unreachable, the guardians' recovery path (the impersonation quorum) answers, never the local read alone. The reason: no vessel reads a head it never received, and the house keeps no registry of heads; no flip stands, and the residue stays stated: one agreeing party may itself sit stale (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>).
* ''The Offering Antigen scope'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A signed Offering act carries a stable scope, the joint Nexus AID its signed inception mints, beside `sealEpochCid` as the authorizing epoch. The receiver resolves its own charter scope, verifies the act's epoch inside that scope's locally held lineage, checks the quorum at that exact epoch, and only then folds; an act whose scope differs stays as foreign evidence outside the `kapae` and `un_kapae` fold, and an incomplete lineage reads `unavailable`. The joint inception image commits to the offer fingerprint and to both founders' fresh cold successor commitments, and a successor commitment a vault already used refuses, so the AID names one founding statement and two foundings by one roster never alias. The reason: a neighbour's condemnation copied onto another board reads as testimony, never as that board's verdict. The ruling flips on a case where two foundings by one roster must read as one Nexus (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/offering-antigen-scope-decision-record">>).
* ''The fork-resolve act'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A fork stays a fork, and every reader names the branch it follows; no clock, no longest chain and no third-party quorum picks a branch. Two acts resolve one. ABANDON: a seal record a branch's own quorum signs at its head, closing that branch for every reader who holds it, erasing nothing and touching no sibling. REUNION: one canonical image naming the scope AID, both held heads sorted, and the new seats, threshold and successor commitments, signed by each branch's quorum at its head over identical bytes; the verifier proves both heads inside the same AID's lineages from a common ancestor, and the record becomes the common head. A seal record's predecessors form a sorted set of one or two, built before the fence; until the reunion verifier lands, a two-predecessor record refuses `REUNION_UNBUILT`. The reason: a split household reunites only by a paper both halves sign. The ruling flips on a fork that neither branch's quorum can still sign for.
* ''The re-seal proof'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). When a content key rolls at a counted removal, the face session that writes each replacement signs one causal re-seal record per replacement: the bag locator, the old and new key-version ids, the old and new ciphertext addresses, the logical entry it replaces and the removal act that authorized the roll. The removal quorum signs the roll, never each body. A reader with partial history verifies the signature and every binding it holds, and reports a missing parent as unverified, never as complete. The format lands with sealed-store step three, before the fence. The reason: a phone offline through the roll needs to know which photo replaced which, and whose hand re-sealed it. The ruling flips on proof that the key-version record, manifests and checkpoints already show every replacement and omission to both kinds of reader (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store">>).
* ''Guardian enrolment's shape'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each face founds one-of-one with no guardians, and its log's verifier knows a typed first-enrolment event from the start: the face's own current key signs it, it commits the guardian set and threshold, and the face's prefix stays as it stands; every later change to the set takes the guardians' authority. The event shape and its verifier land before the fence, so an enrolment never re-founds a face. The reason: a face founded alone keeps its Handle's history when guardians join a year later, and no third hand holds standing over a one-of-one face. The ruling flips on an existing event kind that already permits that first, face-key-signed transition. Guardian count, request authentication, the veto, death and succession stay open (#/open).
* ''Engine cadence'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Cadence keeps its own lineage and threshold, apart from every seat key. A Nexus's joint founding cites one cadence inception that names each founder's hearth, the device that bears the reboot, as a holder; a household seed carries none, since its one holder bears its reboots. Each hearth mints its cadence key at random on the device, never derived from serving or floor material, and keeps it, with its next-key commitment, as a `cold` row under the vessel key that unwraps per act; no sixth custody class and no extra card at the founding. A holder may later rotate onto a cold card by its own choice. Each island pins one engine epoch, and one daemon hosts two epochs only as two separately pinned islands. The reason: the people who bear a reboot decide it, and a seat-holder who runs no hardware forces no one's restart. The ruling flips on a cadence act that must run unattended where a per-act unwrap cannot (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/contract">>).
* ''A realm's quorum and its boards'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each realm stands under ONE Nexus, which the vessel's first act there names and its edge ledger keeps; every reader takes the realm's quorum roster off that Nexus's charter, never off its own primary one; and the realm's boards key on the realm's own id, so a removal's record stays among its parties. The reason: authority comes from the governing charter while the record addresses the relation it affects. The ruling flips on a realm-native charter model with its own seat creation, migration and conflict rule (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/edge-hands">>).
* ''A restore after guardian enrolment'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A face's own bundle restores after it enrols guardians, so long as the face's log still holds the set its own root enrolled by the typed first event and no later event moved it; a guardian rotation that moves the set cuts every earlier bundle off. The restored face stands provisional as the restore's key-history ruling holds. The reason: enrolling guardians must never silently kill a person's own backups, and the guardians' rotation, not the enrolment, cuts off a stolen bundle. The ruling flips on a provisional gate that cannot stop a restored face from signing an authority-changing act before a party confirms it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>).
* ''Paper restores a root, never memory'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). The split-root cards rebuild the persona root alone; content keys stay random and wrap under the vessel key or a bundle's own key, and no key the root derives opens memory. A person recovers memory through a bundle, under the route that bundle seals. The reason: a root card that opened memory would turn every share holder and every thief of the paper into a reader of the face's private bytes. The ruling flips on an explicit choice that root-share holders read memory, with the custody that choice costs stated.
* ''A restore as persona zero'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A restore onto a fresh hearth runs through the founding act: the hearth founds a fresh one-seat seed charter, and the restored face stands as its persona zero and holds its new chair. A bundle's old chair claim carries no authority on the new hearth, since a bundle carries no seat key. A founding that stopped part way resumes only its own persona-zero root and its own seed charter, and refuses a root that differs from the restored one rather than minting over it. The reason: a person who lost their one machine meets their own face, never a second one. The ruling flips on a rule that a lost hearth's chair must survive, which would make a bundle carry seat authority it excludes.
* ''A roll waits on its prior anchor'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-15). A seal roll refuses by name while the prior roll stands with no anchor, so no Nexus stacks a roll over a step its readers cannot yet carry across a removal. The reason: an admit carries across a seal roll only through its roll anchors, and a roll laid over an anchorless one strands every admit between them.
* ''LAN candidate discovery'': the module and wiring stand off by default; `LAR_LAN_DISCOVERY=announce` enables announcements without keys. A real-LAN run and the network mode remain owed (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/lan-discovery`).
* ''Household HTTPS'': the compose `https` profile supplies a terminator and a resolver slot, the `issuer` profile supplies a one-shot DNS-01 issuer that reads the registrar's credential from two owner-read files outside both mounts (`docker-compose.surface.yml`; `tools/household-https-witness.sh` walks issuance against a test authority), the resolver answers each household name with its own address from one records file the operator keeps (`tools/household-resolver-witness.sh` walks it against a test zone), and `deviceAdmitSyncUrl` derives relay reach from `LAR_PUBLIC_URL`. The node entry names itself by that setting alone: a boot without one, or with one that names loopback, says so in one loud line, and the first request that arrives under another host logs once per boot (`packages/lararium-node/src/reach-face-watch.ts`). The operator supplies the domain and the credential files. Owed: the renewal verb with its reload, and a first live issuance. No device takes an install (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/household-https`, `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context`).
* ''The harnesses stand on the image'': harnesses run the built image by digest and bind no source tree; the mesh scenarios name their own compose project (`MESH_PROJECT`) and feed their browser vessels from the image; the browser CI runner alone reads the repository read-only. Each harness opens the vessel it staged through the test custody door and reads a hearth as stood only once that door answered an open key; `mesh-scenarios all`, `harbor-walk-witness`, the crossing and herm witnesses, the browser walks and the household HTTPS witness all stand on that shape (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/the-harnesses`).
* ''The release manifest'': `tools/release-manifest.mjs` emits the record shape; a real multi-arch index, its push and signing remain owed.

!!! 4 · KAHU PLANE ~ the realms, the signer, and the one AID train
Build order: the realm and signer shapes against the rulings; then R-CHARTER, the one train that moves
identity bytes; then R-WIRE, so every pin changes once.

* ''Realms'': a vessel stands one plane per realm, and a verb names its realm (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/many-realms">>). A cabal reads as its relation closure and takes no key or roster of its own. A cabal realm's documents cross between two operators once the realm's member consult answers for a peer.
* ''The steward signer'': the worn face or a quorum Handle, never the root (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-steward-signer">>). The seal, the fold and the steward door stand in code; the composing vessel's `realm-bag` verb takes the door through the compose owner's hand. The docker `meme` scenario then proves it by hand at "B edits, promotes", in the asker's order: B asks to keep the bag with her worn face, and A consents.
* ''Thresholded admission and promotion'' stand built: the chartered classes derive from the charter's own seats and threshold (`chartered-classes.ts`); promotion approves the exact bytes' hash with purpose, surface, class and epoch (`promotion-approval.ts`, `lares nexus promote open|sign|check`); Kahu-cabal admission stands as its own act at the high-impact threshold, where a vouch admits nobody (`kahu-cabal-admission.ts`, `lares nexus cabal`) (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/chartered-classes">>).

''R-CHARTER, the one AID train.'' Its first stage stands landed (train K of #/the-trains): the chairs sign with seat keys of their own and pre-rotate on cold cards, a Nexus answers to `nexus-<sha>`, the floor key commits no next key, per-Nexus keys mint at random, the PersonaGroup stands as a Keyhive group, and the Stable founding act founds a hearth (`lares vessel found --hearth`): the place, persona zero and a PRIVATE seed charter at one seat, with no keyring, every fleet relation bound to that charter's AID, and every harness that founds a hearth founding through it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/found">>). What stays owed:
* ''The Nexus founding act'': two founders, each from their own vault, seat one key each in a fresh joint genesis at two of two; each reads the other's seat fingerprint aloud; each seat commits its own cold next digest; each founder then signs a carriage contract that both seats countersign (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>). Train N.
* ''The cadence inception'' cited once by a joint Nexus founding, never by a household seed: its shape stands in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/contract">>, and its holders stand ruled (#/rulings; `K9`).
* ''Boards resolve per relation''; the fleet-join door carries a device edge and one face's grants; add-a-face (A); the bare primary-path seal import goes; the mirror guard keys on posture.

''R-WIRE, the per-Nexus wire key'' (waits on R-CHARTER).
