bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:845:''LEAD sequencing:'' K15.1 define exhaustive row schema and independent FCK/slot/recovery tree; K15.2 enforce per-face identity ownership and split mixed carriers; K15.3 bind browser tab/session and node session to one face, apply (a′), land F6 slice three with authenticated occupied-row replacement; K15.4 remove one face and its rows without sibling rotation; K15.5 one-face recovery/restore; K15.6 migrate identity bytes/stores/FCK routes/archive in one fence. F6 routes writes over established key ownership; it cannot create isolation over VK. Keep Herm's private place signer decision and container no-human restart behavior explicit prerequisites. `sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-309` shows current occupied-row replacement authenticates existing ciphertext and verifies before rename; preserve that refusal/atomicity in the face writer.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:948:For every row-bearing node/container type above, acts are filtered from the merged census: (1) found place / VK / slot tree → place signing seed ''S'' under today's implementation; public place/card/charter F; each PersonaGroup root/FCK/slot tree D; serving key/tree only becomes S if a serving payload is actually present; (2) fleet offer/join pending → public signed transport F, target face's pending secret/address/record D; (3) slot bind/remove/recovery → that face's route tree D; (4) roster, labels, declarations, handle-book → face-specific content D, minimal opaque public routing only F; (5) Keyhive/Veil archive, dyads, face proof/member and Nexus keys → D; (6) face backup/restore → D; (7) clear custody removes that face's rows and routes, while F survives; (8) floor card, public charter, public gate and public artifact carriage → F. Prior precise carrier/caller citations: `../PRIOR-YIN.md` census table and `../PRIOR-YANG.md` census table. Node carrier line inventory: `vault-carriers.ts:287-398`; rotation's current VK-carrier sweep: `vault-carriers.ts:477-510` (current mechanic to be replaced by face/row-aware rotation).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1013:For every node/container/seat type in the previous table, common acts filter as follows (''MEASURED current carriers; LEAD target rows''): founding place signing seed is S on node (public verifying half/card/charter F); PersonaGroup root, FCK and slot tree D; fleet public signed offer/answer F while target-face pending secret/address/join record D; face slot changes/recovery D; private roster, labels, handle-book/recognition graph D while minimal public opaque routes F only after leakage review; Keyhive/Veil archives, dyads, proof/member/Nexus keys D; per-face backup/restore D; clear-custody removes only that face's D and leaves F; future serving rows S only when instantiated and named. The carrier/caller evidence appears in `../PRIOR-YIN.md` and `../PRIOR-YANG.md` census tables and `packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:287-398`; archive row selector remains unresolved.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1107:''LEAD recommendation: split a long-lived place root from a boot signer, and give the place identifier a KERI-like pre-rotated recovery path.'' The online signer may sign a tightly enumerated set of place acts and public floor-gate derivations. An offline owner-held root or threshold-held next key authorizes rotation of the online signer and publishes a verifiable KEL event. Keep at least one next key outside the always-on node. Use TPM/systemd encrypted credential as an optional at-rest wrapper for the online signer on supported nodes, with explicit fallback policy on systems lacking TPM.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1109:This third shape improves on plain clear-file candidate (1) by making stale online-key theft recoverable and machine-tied at rest where available. It improves on human-at-every-boot candidate (2) by preserving Herm public service and unattended container restart. It does ''not'' erase the theft window: attacker with current signer can forge until verifiers accept the rotation; attacker with next/root key can seize or permanently lose the place identity. If recovery cannot be implemented with independently protected next key, do not claim pre-rotation protection. Root/next compromise in pure KERI can require abandoning the identifier and re-founding identity with ecosystem-level rebinding.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1124:Detection: compare KEL histories / witnessed public floor records, keep tamper-evident receipts and independent public-key pins, and alert on conflicting valid histories or unexpected rotation. Recovery: rotate place key through authenticated KEL pre-rotation if next key remains safe; revoke/replace relying pins and re-found/rebind if recovery authority is also lost. Kapae/re-found semantics are house-specific and were not independently verified in this internet pass, so this mapping is ''LEAD'', not a measured procedure. A stolen current key enables forgery until rotation is accepted; pre-rotation does not retroactively erase that window.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1146:# ''Third shape: named place signer + separately protected offline/threshold place root and pre-rotated next key; optionally TPM/systemd-wrap online signer.'' Best blend for unattended public availability and recoverability. It still recognizes an online signer as a limited place authority. Herm/node can start at floor; a compromised online seed is revocable only while next/root authority is safe.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1164:Build one end-to-end adversarial test around a copied data disk and a no-human reboot: (a) on approved machine/boot, public place card and public relay start; every exact place signer domain is logged against an allowlist; (b) neither `vesselSeed` nor derived private child bytes appear in disk image, environment, logs, API, or public artifacts; (c) a different host cannot unwrap the credential; (d) no VK/FCK is present and direct attempts to open, replace, restore, rotate, or decrypt any face row fail byte-preservingly; (e) same-host restart does not admit a face; (f) attack-simulate theft of the current place signer and show an independently held pre-rotated key emits a valid KEL rotation, after which old-key place acts are rejected; (g) compromise of the offline next key is treated as identity-loss/re-found, not claimed recoverable. For candidate (2), add the expected deliberate tradeoff assertion: no person means no signer and no signed place identity at boot.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1241:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' The place KEL offers no recovery: `vessel-kel.ts:2-13` and `node-vessel-identity.ts:185-198` explicitly define non-transferable identity, no next-key commitment, and fresh founding after loss/theft. There is no place KEL rotation path in the assigned source; `rg` found only inception/decode/verify. `lares nexus seal rotate` rotates charter seats/epoch, not the place key. Vault `--rotate` rotates custody VK, not this seed.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1242:* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' With current non-rotatable KEL, recovery means abandon/re-found the place identity and re-pin/re-carry it through the relevant Nexus, fleet, and floor-gate relationships. The board can detect signatures that fail, but cannot distinguish attacker signatures made with the stolen valid seed from the real place. Kapae can shadow relationships/edges under their own authorities; it does not recover the compromised place identity or revoke every consumer automatically. Re-found carries continuity and coordination costs.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1253:* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' A genuinely useful split would put a long-term place root offline and have it certify replaceable boot keys, with explicit expiry/revocation/recovery authority; merely certifying one static boot signer does not help if that signer cannot be revoked. But this changes all verifiers/pins and recovery semantics. A pre-rotation place KEL could make compromise recoverable only if the next private key stays outside the stolen boundary and all consumers enforce the chain.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1254:* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' For the existing code contract, prefer a narrowly named place-signer S protected at rest and auto-available for public floor, while explicitly excluding VK/FCK and documenting non-rotatable identity/re-found recovery. This is a decision recommendation, not a claim that code already meets the protection standard.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1272:# ''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — Rank 1: named place signer, protected at rest, auto-loaded for floor only.'' Keep the floor free of custody VK/FCK; name the signer as a place-identity capability, constrain its APIs to the existing place/floor domains, and protect the seed with an owner-only file at minimum or an OS/TPM keystore where it can actually boot unattended. Document the remaining host-compromise risk and mandatory re-found recovery under today's KEL. This best preserves current herm availability, though code currently uses the signer beyond a narrow signing API (including derivation/group-secret opener), so implementation must narrow/justify each of those uses.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1273:# ''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — Rank 2: split offline root plus replaceable boot signer.'' This offers the strongest recoverability if KEL/verifiers/pins gain a root-certified rotation chain and next authority stays offline. It has a larger migration and compatibility burden; it outranks a simple unlock door for deployments that can operate a carefully provisioned online key.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1343:| ''Signal'' | Signal's key-transparency service uses log/index structures and independent auditors to detect inconsistent views of user key mappings; PQXDH puts user identity/prekeys on server while private keys stay with user. [[Automatic Key Verification|https://signal.org/blog/automatic-key-verification/]], [[PQXDH spec|https://signal.org/docs/specifications/pqxdh/]]. | Analogy for witnessing public place-key history and keeping face keys separate. Transparency detects equivocation; it does not prevent place-seed theft or itself authorize recovery. |
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1356:''MEASURED — 2026-10-19 — twin-alone:'' Existing place KEL provides no next-key commitment, rotation, or recovery. Therefore it offers no cryptographic way to distinguish legitimate from attacker signatures made with the same stolen seed. Independent transparency witnesses and authenticated pins could reveal inconsistent public histories, but are not in the measured place KEL.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1367:''Rank 1 — LEAD — 2026-10-19 — confirmed-by-both: third shape, split root and boot signer with pre-rotated recovery.'' Keep an owner/threshold-controlled place root and committed next key offline; use a replaceable, narrowly scoped online place signer for unattended floor operations. Optionally protect its at-rest credential with TPM/systemd or OS keystore where available. Publish and verify a KEL-like rotation chain across all consumers. This improves theft recovery while preserving service. It is not a quick wrapper change: existing KEL and relying pins do not support this today, and current signer consumers must be narrowed/audited first.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1387:Run an adversarial no-human boot and copied-disk test. On the authorized host, a faceless Herm and restarted container must publish the expected place card/relay/gate identity; no seed or child secret appears in disk-visible public artifacts, logs, environment, or API. On a copied disk / wrong host, signer release fails if TPM/host binding was selected (or the documented owner-only file exposure is demonstrated if not). With no VK/FCK, attempts to read, replace, restore, rotate, or decrypt any face row fail byte-preservingly. Verify allowlisted signer domains only, and specifically assert that group-secret and dyad derivations cannot become broad custody opens. Then simulate current online-key theft: offline pre-rotated authority emits a valid successor; all consumers advance and reject old-key place acts. If offline next/root is also compromised, require re-found rather than claiming recovery. Candidate (2) variant must assert that no human means no signed place floor identity.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1433:''MEASURED 2026-10-19:'' The system analogies describe at-rest and boot-release controls; none grants Lares row-key authority. The current mesh floor KEL does not possess KERI pre-rotation. Its format has exactly one self-signed `icp` key and rejects next-key commitment (`vessel-kel.ts:1-17,38-45`). Thus KERI's recovery guarantee cannot be mapped onto today's place key. Nexus charter/seat rotation and Kapae do not repair the place identity: they govern charter seats/participants, not floor KEL. Signal-like public transparency could help detect conflicting announcements, but no place witness/transparency path surfaced in the audited code.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1437:''LEAD 2026-10-19:'' Map systemd encrypted credentials/TPM to wrapping the place signer at rest and unattended boot release. This may reduce copied-disk theft while preserving Herm uptime; it does not address compromise of a running host. Vault auto-unseal and Kubernetes secret mounts resemble delegated machine release and have similar runtime trust; plain Kubernetes Secret is not enough. Tor/SSH/WireGuard/ACME show how operators accept an always-on service identity and respond to theft by replacing/rebinding public identity. KERI pre-rotation is the closest recovery design only after adding the absent place-KEL machinery. Signal transparency is useful detection inspiration, not key protection.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1446:''Rank 1 — third shape: split long-lived place root from scoped boot signer, with independently protected pre-rotated successor. LEAD, 2026-10-19.'' Keep Herm's operational signer online, narrowly authorized for explicitly named floor/place domains. Let an offline owner-held root or threshold process authorize successor rotation; keep next seed off host. Optionally wrap online signer with TPM/systemd credential on supported systems. First remove or separately justify non-place uses (notably dyad veil/face enrollment). This preserves unattended public boot and adds a recovery path; it does not prevent forgery while a current signer remains accepted. Requires new KEL contract, verifier, rotation command, witness/pin update, and recovery runbook.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1459:''Red test (LEAD, 2026-10-19):'' fresh zero-face Herm boot and unattended container restart must serve public card/charter and derive only allowlisted floor-gate pins; no VK/FCK/SK or face row opens. Read copied signer file and show attacker can forge current place assertion, then rotate through separately held next authority and prove old key fails while new KEL verifies; attempted floor-door sovereign derivation and cross-face decrypt must refuse. Repeat on rank 2 without offline rotation to demonstrate expected permanent-key recovery gap; rank 3 must demonstrably refuse signer-backed endpoints before human unlock while leaving public unsigned bytes readable.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/the-crux-first-secret-sharing-vs-independent-d6ddd5.mem:391:**The generalization: nobody does threshold *signing*.** Every archive is signed by one online key. Where m-of-n appears it is in one of two adjacent places — **recovering** a lost secret (Debian 3-of-5) or **admitting** a key to the trust set (Debian 2 DDs, kernel.org 2 account holders, Arch 3 master keys). And every recovery in the record has the same shape: *mint a new key, get humans to sign it out of band, re-sign or re-point the archive, and push the new anchor through the old key if it still works — by hand if it doesn't.*
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/0-method-and-the-honest-limit-on-this-report-52ebe0.mem:369:- **Google Recovery Contacts** (Oct 16, 2025): up to **10 trusted contacts** per account; a locked-out user shares a numeric code; the contact number-matches it against three options; **15-minute expiry**; each person may serve as recovery contact for up to **25 accounts**; unavailable for Workspace and child accounts; Advanced Protection members may help others but not set their own. Google still layers device/location/IP heuristics on top and may still hold the account. Named risk: social-engineering the contact — **which is precisely the §5.2 UI-bound-adversary problem arriving at recovery time.**
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/0-method-and-the-honest-limit-on-this-report-52ebe0.mem:640:- **Google Recovery Contacts** (Oct 16, 2025, The Register): up to **10 trusted contacts** per account; a locked-out user shares a numeric code; the contact number-matches it against three options; **15-minute expiry**; each person may be a recovery contact for up to **25 accounts**; unavailable for Workspace and child accounts; APP participants may help others but not set their own. Google still layers device/location/IP heuristics on top and may still hold the account. Named risk: social-engineering the contact.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:48:  - The mail does **not** state the old 2003 key was on a compromised box; it presents the new key as part of recovery. *Confidence that the roll was caused by the compromise: high (timing + placement in the recovery mail). Confidence that the old private key was actually exposed: low — never stated.*
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:183:**The generalization worth carrying:** across every system examined, package/archive signing is **single-key custody with one signature**. Nobody does threshold *signing*. Where m-of-n appears, it is in one of two adjacent places — (a) **recovering** a lost secret (Debian's 3-of-5 Shamir), or (b) **admitting** a key to the trust set (Debian's 2 DD signatures, kernel.org's 2 account-holder signatures, Arch's 3 master signatures). The signing operation itself is always a single online holder, and the recovery path in every incident above was the same shape: *mint a new key, get it signed by humans out of band, re-sign or re-point the archive, and push the new trust anchor to users through the old one if it still works — or by hand if it doesn't.*
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:210:- **The negative result, and it is load-bearing:** **the DPS does not specify what happens if a Crypto Officer or a Recovery Key Share Holder becomes unavailable or leaves.** It mentions "Job Rotation Frequency and Sequence" and says only *"Other positions are rotated and replaced as needed."* No succession procedure. **In the world's most audited key ceremony, the "guardian is unreachable" case is unwritten.** And as far as the public record shows, **the 5-of-7 recovery path has never been exercised.**
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:295:**R9.6 has a subtlety worth carrying.** ICN found the contest window **cannot be position-denominated** — *"in a takeover the attacker controls log advancement and would simply advance past any positional window."* It must be **wall-clock**, and they accept the consequence honestly: *"two nodes of the same institution with identical durable state but skewed clocks can reach different verdicts during the window… **This is the only place in the design where a receiver's clock decides anything.**"* Argent reached the same conclusion empirically (36h/48h wall-clock windows), and Schechter's data says a 48h window covers **88%** of successful guardian recoveries.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:457:- 2024-06-09: **the 2FA server itself was compromised.** The attacker *"temporarily replaced affected users' 2FA data with their own email addresses,"* initiated social recovery, received the code, and Loopring's relayer *"signed 'YES' to the social recovery request."* **The attacker did not defeat recovery — they invoked it, exactly as designed.**
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:480:Launched 2011, relaunched 2013, ~**2 billion accounts**, 3-friend confirmation. **Removed 2022.** Facebook's own help-page notice, verbatim: *"Trusted contacts is going away soon. This means your chosen friends will no longer be able to help you get back on Facebook if you lose access to your account."* Replacement advice: keep your email and phone current. **Meta ran the largest possible experiment in social recovery for ~9 years, published no results, and shut it down.** *(This supersedes my earlier 16/20 — the verbatim notice was retrieved.)*
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:608:> **The generalization: nobody does threshold *signing*.** Every archive is signed by one online key. Where m-of-n appears it is in one of two adjacent places — **recovering** a lost secret (Debian 3-of-5), or **admitting** a key to the trust set (Debian 2 DDs, kernel.org 2 account holders, Arch 3 master keys). And every recovery in the record has the same shape: **mint a new key, get humans to sign it out of band, re-sign or re-point the archive, and push the new anchor through the old key if it still works — by hand if it doesn't.**
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:647:Across five research streams, **not one documented failure in the entire public record turned on Shamir-versus-multisig.** They turned on: quorum counts that weren't counts of distinct signers (four sightings, one a recurrence in the same library six years later); custody that was correlated in ways nobody re-checked (Ronin's 5-of-9 that was 1-of-1, Loopring's guardians-of-guardians, FNMT's access codes stored beside the HSM); ceremonies whose script and reality diverged undetected for six months to six years; guardians who forgot, went dark, or folded to a warm phone call; and vendors who left. **The two forks differ most in a place neither branch names: Fork A's assembled secret is a bearer token that can be couriered, delegated, and improvised with — which is simultaneously its worst security property and the only reason ICANN could hold a ceremony during a pandemic. Fork B cannot bend, in either direction.** And the finding that changes the shape rather than answering it: under KERI's own rules, **a non-delegated rotation cannot supersede another rotation** — so whichever fork you take, a compromise of the recovery authority is terminal unless the identifier is delegated. That is a third axis, and on severity it dominates the one you asked about.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/1-cohost-anti-software-software-club-7e9c48.mem:186:**NOT REACHED**: a primary Path source announcing the 50→150 raise, or giving a reason. I fetched the Path 2.0 announcement ("Introducing Path 2, the Smart Journal," `blog.path.com/post/13533662902/…`, posted **2011-11-29**, Wayback capture 2012-12-27) in full — **it does not mention the friend limit at all**. Nor does Path 2.5 (2012-07-25), nor `path.com/about` (Wayback 2013-01-14), nor `path.com/story` (Wayback 2013-08-23). The `path.com/questions` FAQ page — which is linked in the 2012–2013 site chrome and would be the place the number lived — **has no Wayback snapshot** under any URL variant I tried. TechCrunch's 2011-11-29 piece (`http://techcrunch.com/2011/11/29/paths-second-iteration-is-less-photosharing-and-more-everything-sharing/`, recovered via HN `objectID 3649...`/story 2011-11-30) now 404s on techcrunch.com.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/1-cohost-anti-software-software-club-7e9c48.mem:232:The design is exactly the inversion you're studying: a reader-authored, subscribable, *public-by-default* grouping layered over an asymmetric follow graph — and note that "public by default" makes the curation itself a social object, which is why Lists became a research instrument (below).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/2-dns-root-system-41-years-measured-resolver-c48256.mem:196:5. **Replication was allowed to grow without renegotiating the protocol.** The root went from 13 machines to **2,005 anycast instances under the same 13 identities** — capacity multiplied ~154× with zero protocol change and zero new governance seats. The DNS root's decentralization is entirely an *operational* achievement layered under a fixed, small, politically frozen namespace.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/who-paid-for-volunteer-run-relay-infrastruct-aa2e60.mem:106:**Exit scarcity**: exits fell from 46.4% of relays (2007) to **11.7% (2017)**, the measured floor — the legally-exposed role is the one volunteers refuse. It has since recovered to ~29.7%, driven by a handful of professionalized non-profits (below), not by dispersion.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/final-report-device-per-person-ground-truth-14552e.mem:61:**Global mobile subscriptions** (Wikipedia "Mobile phone," sourced): 9.1B subscriptions by 2024 (more than world population), but only ~7.2B people (roughly half the world's countries counted) actually own at least one phone — subscriptions overcount because of the multi-SIM/multi-device population layered on top of the zero-device population.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/social-guardian-key-recovery-as-actually-pra-e9aa59.mem:109:- **Incident #2, 2024-06-09:** the **Loopring 2FA server** was compromised (full read/write). The attacker *"temporarily replaced affected users' 2FA data with their own email addresses"*, initiated social recovery, received the code, and Loopring's relayer *"signed 'YES' to the social recovery request"* — transferring ownership on-chain, **exactly as designed**.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/social-guardian-key-recovery-as-actually-pra-e9aa59.mem:148:**Vitalik's 2021 post** — https://vitalik.eth.limo/general/2021/01/11/recovery.html, 2021-01-11. *HIGH.* Recommends *"high guardian counts (ideally 7+)"*, 1–3 day delay on guardian changes. Loss motivation: *"1500 BTC may be lost every day"*, *"20% of the total supply"*, Stefan Thomas's 7,000 BTC, allinvain's 25,000 BTC. Names as risks: guardian collusion, relayer centralization, high L1 fees, guardian identity disclosure. Example: *"for a wallet with 7 guardians to be compromised, 4 of the 7 guardians would need"* to collude.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/social-guardian-key-recovery-as-actually-pra-e9aa59.mem:348:- Incident #2, **2024-06-09**: the **Loopring 2FA server** was compromised (read/write). The attacker **swapped affected users' 2FA email addresses for their own**, initiated social recovery, received the verification code, and Loopring's relayer **"signed 'YES' to the social recovery request"** — transferring wallet ownership to the attacker on-chain, exactly as designed.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/social-guardian-key-recovery-as-actually-pra-e9aa59.mem:383:**Vitalik's 2021 post itself**, https://vitalik.eth.limo/general/2021/01/11/recovery.html, **2021-01-11**: recommends **"high guardian counts (ideally 7+)"**, a 1–3 day delay on guardian changes, and cites the loss motivation ("1500 BTC may be lost every day", "20% of the total supply", Stefan Thomas's 7,000 BTC). It names guardian collusion, relayer centralization, high L1 fees, and guardian-identity disclosure as risks. **Note the gap between "ideally 7+" and Argent's shipped default of 1.** *Confidence: HIGH.*
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/social-guardian-key-recovery-as-actually-pra-e9aa59.mem:465:**11. The vendor exits.** Facebook's Trusted Contacts — the largest social-recovery deployment in history, launched 2011 as "Trusted Friends", ~2 billion accounts — **was removed in 2022**. Facebook's own help-page notice, quoted in Ask Leo's contemporaneous coverage (https://askleo.com/facebook-trusted-contacts-is-going-away-prepare-now/, ~May 2022; corroborated by azcentral 2022-05-30 and Screen Rant): ***"Trusted contacts is going away soon. This means your chosen friends will no longer be able to help you get back on Facebook if you lose access to your account."*** Their replacement advice: keep your email and phone number current. **Meta ran the largest possible experiment in social recovery, ran it for roughly nine years, published no results, and shut it down.** *Confidence: HIGH on the removal and the quote; MEDIUM on the exact date.*
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/research-report-identity-as-dyad-person-devi-f55eee.mem:63:**KERI** (Sam Smith, arXiv 1907.02143, "Key Event Receipt Infrastructure") is a different and arguably more rigorous model: identity is a **self-certifying identifier bound to a key-event log** (append-only, signed transfer statements, pre-rotation, witnessed for duplicity detection). This is not quite "identity as an edge between two parties" — it's "identity as a *history*," verified independently by any witness, with **direct mode** (1:1 relationships via verified signatures) and **indirect mode** (1:many via witnessed receipts) as two separate trust postures layered on top. So KERI keeps identity subject-centric (one controller, one log) but makes trust *relational at the verification layer*. Citation count on the arXiv paper is only **2** (OpenAlex) — this is a spec/protocol effort, not an academic one, so citation count undercounts real influence (it lives inside Trust-over-IP governance work instead), but it is a low signal for independent scholarly uptake. (Confidence: medium.)
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/heraldry.mem:110:''Signed by the Herm's OWN vessel key, device-minted.'' Not a persona root (the class forbids one, `vessel-standing.ts:69-70`), not the relay gate key (that one derives domain-separated for the transport and stays a transport identity — `carriage-relay.ts:60-77`; joining them would rebuild the link the veil model spends its design preventing, `carriage-relay.ts:62-65`). The ''Place DID'' fills the owner-set: `mintHandleInceptionSet(handleKeyDid, [placeDid], 1, recoverySetHash)` — the same call `mintHandleInception` already sugars for a 1-of-1 (`handle-kel.ts:228-237`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/recovery-custody-scout.mem:127:* ''kernel.org'' — ''no archive key at all''. The web of trust IS the recovery mechanism: ≥2 existing-member signatures re-admit a re-keyed developer. Its governing principle: //"trust must always be placed with developers and never with the code hosting infrastructure."// The 2011 recovery required ''in-person'' signatures.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/recovery-custody-scout.mem:196:* ''Four claims were corrected in place mid-sweep'', by the spirit against itself — including a material error in the social-recovery figures and a wrong claim that ICANN never wrote down the unavailable-shareholder case. ''A report that corrects itself twice is worth more than one that never does''; read the corrections as the instrument working.
