99:The worn face is SESSION state: no `active-persona` row rests (the person names the face at each unlock, `lares vault unlock --face N`).
101:Rows whose class includes ''LEAD'' block byte movement until their payload and owning principal are proved. Current `face` and `bundle` fields describe filename/export selection only (`vault-carriers.ts:119-165`); they never confer cryptographic separation. The existing S-key rotation sweeps all hot/cold rows (`:64-68,477-510`), so K15 replaces it with per-face and named-S rotation.
105:<<~ ahu #/row-census-browser>>
107:!!! Row census — browser
110:The exact `CUSTODY_ROW` alternatives are at `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts:54-68`. They currently share one `custody-slots` tree and one page-held S key for sealed writes (`:2-12,95,386-402`). `catalog-url` is explicitly outside that matcher and public (`:5-13`). The target has no browser S key and no browser S.
113:| `vessel-key` | ''F/D split:'' public vessel contact/routing material F; any face identity seed D by its PersonaGroup. Retire the browser common private device seed and S key. ''LEAD:'' audit consumers of current seed before deletion. |
115:| `veil-archive-g<digest>` | ''D:'' the node rests each face's veil archive under the SHA-256 digest of its PersonaGroup, so the row names its face by construction; the browser's `veil-archive-h<N>` stays index-keyed until its per-face social planes stand. |
124:The browser's clear-before-slot allowance for `vessel-key`, `keyhive-archive`, and `veil-archive-h<N>` (`browser-custody.ts:390-395`) does not apply to target D. Every face secret needs its own slot tree before first persistence, including first fleet join (`stable-founding-road.mem:200`). `tm-clear-password` currently clears the common tree and rows (`browser-custody.ts:34-39,613-650`); target clear/removal acts name one face and cannot clear another.
133:Add this row beside the key table at `bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store.mem#/keys` (`:60-73`):
136:| ''Face custody key (FCK)'' | One PersonaGroup's admitted, unlocked face session; on browser, only the authorized tab's sealer holds bare key material | That face's custody rows: root, Keyhive/Veil archive, face proof/member and recovery material, face-private roster/book/join/outbox partitions. It does not replace per-bag content keys (`sealed-store.mem:65-72`). | Fresh random key, wrapped only in that face's independent slot/recovery tree; bare only during authorized live use. Never wrapped by S key, a node S key, another face, or a common Keyhive archive. | That face's passphrase, paper route, or measured PRF route; each browser tab asks for its own face unlock. A node face act separately proves its face. | Removing a face revokes its slots/admission and retires its local rows without rotating siblings; old offline bundles and already learned plaintext remain readable to their holders. A one-face recovery route restores only that face's FCK and rows. |
138:The per-face slot tree carries wrapped FCK, slot identifiers and nonsecret KDF parameters; it cannot embed a sibling wrapper. Bind/unbind/rotation prove the standing FCK and affect that tree and that face's rows alone. The bundle contains only intended wrapped recovery routes, not a bare FCK or guardian-held secret shares. A passkey/PRF slot stands only after its derivation is measured on target devices (`browser-custody.ts:41-43`). Last browser tab/session close drops bare face keys and returns to F (`stable-founding-road.mem:199,202`); an installed app or worker does not extend authority.
140:''Node S register.'' The present private place signing seed is a named candidate S, with the two unresolved treatments below (`vault-carriers.ts:240-242`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442`). The existing SK tree has *zero* serving payload rows (`vault-carriers.ts:12-14,249-251`), so SK is not a justification for a common unlock. Future ember serving rows enter S only with their own row names, principal, opener and restart rule. No browser, phone, installed web app, tablet, CLI, MCP, watcher or probe owns S (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`). A container inherits the node's actual S set and cannot create one by packaging.
149:''LEAD target wire format:'' each D seal authenticates one canonical context containing a format/domain name and version, vessel or store namespace, immutable PersonaGroup ID, row name, row kind, and the complete logical row reference. Encode fields unambiguously as length-prefixed bytes, analogous to the sealed value context at `sealed-store.mem:85-106`. Supply the expected context from the authenticated row selector on open; never trust a ciphertext's claimed face. A carrier copied into another face's slot, a different row name, or a different kind fails authentication. F and S use distinct domain names; an S row binds its named node principal and row kind. New names/formats refuse old ciphertext rather than treating it as clear. ''LEAD:'' exact byte layout and migration version receive implementation review before K15.6; these fields and refusal behavior are normative now.
151:Generalize `writeSealedCarrier` from `(key, tree, carrier)` to an explicit `(row class, owner, key, matching tree/door, bound carrier context)`. The caller proves that the key opens the *same owner and row class* against its standing slot tree; an S writer proves its named node door; F has no sealed-carrier writer. Preserve the existing exclusive act, absent-or-authentically-opened standing row rule, and refusal without replacement (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-288`). Seal a full caller-supplied replacement with the same context, write a sidecar, read it back byte-identical, authenticate/open it, then rename (`:290-304`). A failed old-row open, class mismatch, tree mismatch, stale/ambiguous migration shape or failed readback leaves the occupied carrier byte-for-byte unchanged. A whole archive replacement is not an implicit read-modify-write. No encrypt-only capability can perform this overwrite proof. The matching-key rule also governs delete and rotation; never use A's key or node S to authenticate B's standing row.
160:A bundle for PersonaGroup X contains X's root/identity, X's Keyhive and Veil archive, X's selected proof/recovery keys and rows, X's guardian set/commitments, intended wrapped FCK recovery route, X's selected private roster/book lines, and X's content-key memory section and eligible history. It is sealed under the bundle key and its own route (`sealed-store.mem:68`; `vault-carriers.ts:123-165,301-342,377-397`). It never contains a bare FCK, another face's slot or row, another face's identifiers or content keys, a node place signer, a node S key, guardian-held secret shares, or local-only vessel seat/outbox state. `bundle: stays` remains local unless an explicit per-face recovery rule supersedes it. Restore X into a fresh vessel without opening Y, and refuse an ambiguous old mixed archive. Restore does not mint authority over a sibling.
169:# ''Isolation red, every row:'' found A and B with independently random FCKs/trees, occupied D carriers for both, and F plus any named node S values. Unlock A alone. Bypass routers, sessions, UI and store adapters; try direct authenticated open, occupied-row replacement, delete, rotation, backup and restore on *each* B carrier using every A key (FCK, member, identity, content, outbox, recovery), all floor values, and all node S/S key legacy values. Every B operation refuses cryptographically and leaves each B occupied ciphertext byte-for-byte unchanged. Copy A ciphertext into B's slot and swap row name/kind: authentication fails. A's same-row replacement succeeds. Restore A's bundle on a fresh vessel: B identity, key, archive and identifiers are absent; B stays unopened. Remove A: B and F still work. This red is stronger than owner-route refusal (`stable-founding-road.mem:459`; `sealed-writer.ts:279-304`).
170:# ''Browser desktop / laptop:'' two A tabs and one B tab each ask independently; A-caused seal reaches its admitted asker. If that asker cannot answer, only another admitted unlocked A tab may seal; otherwise work names `seal-awaits-session`. Last tab closes, all live FCKs vanish and F remains. A cannot open B even by bypassing holder routing (`holder-routes.ts:23-31`; `shared-holder-core.ts:117-125`; `stable-founding-road.mem:199,202,205`).
171:# ''iOS phone / Home Screen app:'' suspend or kill the asker during an occupied write. The write waits or completes from an eligible same-face session; it never uses a sibling or infers death from silence. Relaunch starts at F and asks for that face again. ''LEAD:'' device lifecycle must be exercised on the target browser (round 4's YIN handback in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">>).
173:# ''Tablet waking floor:'' unattended public/ciphertext sync succeeds; direct D decrypt or transformation fails until that face's session is admitted. The open product requirement for unattended *private* sync cannot silently borrow FCK (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
176:# ''Node daemon / hearth:'' boot with no face admission and no omnibus key; F serves. Admit A and run the isolation red over every B node carrier, including archive, roster, pending join and outbox. Lock/restart drops volatile face keys. Hearth composition grants no extra row principal. Separately verify the place signer only under whichever OPEN JOINT option is ruled (`vault-carriers.ts:239-398`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-487`).
177:# ''Herm / crossroads:'' zero face rows and zero current serving rows; public card/relay service works before any face. No private seed leaks to output. Reboot drops raised ephemeral caps. The place signer's boot behavior follows the OPEN JOINT ruling, not an assumed F classification (`vault-carriers.ts:12-14,240-256`; `stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
182:# ''Generic worker host / LarVessel:'' giving a worker A's context and bypassing its router still fails to open or replace B; worker lifetime changes availability only. Public floor and ciphertext carriage continue without face keys.
191:''K15.0, this gate:'' freeze the exhaustive node/browser row census, prove each split and owner selector, define FCK/tree/recovery and carrier context, specify old-shape refusal and the isolation red. No identity or custody bytes move. ''K15.1:'' use the existing two-hive same-process isolation probe, then give each face its own store, registry, archive and key seat. ''K15.2:'' make the face the sole identity/group owner; capture two-face event bytes and reject any shared creator/issuer. ''K15.3:'' route sessions and registries by exactly one face and land ''F6 slice three'' asker sealing with the correct row-class key and occupied-row proof. ''K15.4:'' separate content-key rolls and removal by face, including K12 fresh group behavior. ''K15.5:'' one-face restore and a herm that carries without face authoring. ''K15.6:'' one migration fence for identity bytes, split stores, FCK slot trees, archives and custody formats; refuse old mixed archives by name. This follows `stable-founding-road.mem:459,507`. ''B1'' consumes browser no-S key, per-tab face asks, slot-before-first-secret and last-tab floor (`:199-202,206-207`). F6 routing does not precede the key boundary.
195:<<~ ahu #/the-floor-index>>
197:!!! The floor index of faces (ruled 2026-10-23)
199:A node with no omnibus key still has to name the faces it can unlock before any face opens, so the identity home keeps ONE F row, `face-index`, in the clear. It lists the node's faces in local face order. Each entry holds the SHA-256 digest of its PersonaGroup (null while the founding stands), the digest of the founding's ask (null once the group stands) and the filename of the face's own slot tree. It holds no name, no handle, no prefix, no label and no key bytes.
203:''The index routes and never decides.'' It rests at F, unauthenticated, so a swapped or tampered entry could point a group at another face's tree. After an unlock, the D anchors row under that face's own FCK names its PersonaGroup; the vessel compares that group with the index entry's digest and refuses by name on a mismatch, before it seats anything. The index never chooses a worn face's authority either: `readFace` reads the sealed anchor.
205:''The owner in every D context is the face custody id (`fcid`),'' a random 16-byte id minted with the face's slot tree at the founding's start. It is immutable and never reused. Rows that exist before the PersonaGroup does (the persona root, the founding's atom ask) seal under it, and so do the rows after, so nothing re-seals when the group arrives. The anchors row binds the fcid to the PersonaGroup. Rows are local and never cross vessels, so the id needs no cross-vessel stability.
218:: ''One child derives today.'' The only call that reaches `holder.derive` is `custodyGateDoor` (`custody-seat.ts`), and its only consumers are `hearthSovereignPin`, `stoodSovereignPin` and the gate composition in `open-node-vessel.ts`: each derives the SOVEREIGN GATE BUNDLE (`GATE_BUNDLE_INFO`) of one Nexus and route. The registry (`ALL_DOMAINS`) declares other domains, and the door derives under any of them, but no other caller derives. The veil, Keyhive signer and hosting key named in the root's header derive from the vessel key, a face's atom or their own rows, never from this root.
219:: ''Owner: the node, not a face.'' The bundle keys the hearth's gate at a Nexus: the node's seat in that Nexus, apart from any PersonaGroup. It is a NODE-ONLY service secret and enters S as the separately named row `gate-root` (the custody root renamed for what it derives). It is never retained as a generic root, and no face derivation hangs from it.
220:: ''Stands:'' `gate-root` rests under the node's S key (the node route), and a derivation under any other domain refuses by name at the door.
223:: ''Owner: the place, not a face.'' The archive is minted at the place card (`ensurePlaceCard`, `init`) from the vessel's own seed before any face stands; a faceless or joinee place holds one. It carries the place Individual's membership and prekey secrets and its contact card. It is a NODE-ONLY secret and enters S as the separately named row `place-archive`. Each face's own archive already rests per PersonaGroup as `veil-archive-g<digest>` (D, ''face-hot'').
224:: ''No identity above the faces.'' The place Individual signs no face's act and holds no face's key; the daemon island restores each face's own veil from that face's FCK row.
230:!!! The place seed's reach (ruled 2026-10-19)
232:* ''The veil leaves the seed.'' `deriveDyadVeil(vesselSeed, groupTag)` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/dyad.ts`) and `groupSecretOpenerFromSeed` (`packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts`) make the vessel seed an ancestor of every face's device veil and group secret. Each becomes D: the veil mints at random at the join and rests under that face's FCK; the opener reads the face's own key. The isolation red gains the row: a thief holding the place seed derives no veil and opens no group secret of any face.
234:* ''The census sorts by capability, never by host'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Each present use of a vessel seed, on node and browser alike, sorts by the capability it serves: a floor duty goes to the place signer, which a vessel holds only while it stands a floor; an act on a face's behalf (a dial, a join, an enrolment, a sibling channel, a daemon proof) goes to that face's atom; nothing else keeps a device-wide key. The browser's device seed (`open-browser-vessel.ts`: the Keyhive Individual, the oracle proof, daemon auth, sibling enrolment, the group-secret opener) and the node's (`open-node-vessel.ts`) take the same sort.
235:* ''The seed's other reach narrows to an allowlist.'' The online place signer serves the floor duties the roadmap's place-signer ruling enumerates; every other present use (the daemon's auth, the device-key signer, the internal asks) either names its domain on that allowlist or takes a key of its own.
241:''The node (S) tree'' is the slot tree `s-slots.bin` wrapping one random S key. It seals exactly two rows: `gate-root` (`gate-root.bin`) and the place archive (`place-archive.bin`). No D row, no face key and no serving key rests under it. The seal frame names it `lares-s-seal`.
251:''The sovereign gate.'' The daemon composes sovereign when S stands held AND the worn face stands unlocked (a place with no face needs S alone); otherwise the floor composes. `sovereignStands()` reads this as a pure query. `lares vault lock --face N` drops one face; locking the worn face, or locking S, stands the vessel back at the floor through the same gate, and a sibling's lock leaves sovereign standing. The founding sweep of seeds runs under the gate only.
255:''Status prints a face as one line:'' its handle index, a custody-id prefix, the count of rows under its key, and the group and ask digests the floor index names. A face's row never prints by name; only a fault does.
262:# ''Node private place signing seed'' (ruled 2026-10-19: a scoped online signer under a pre-rotated place KEL whose next key rides the founding card, built in the founding bake; the options it settled follow). Current `.vessel-key*.json` contains a private signing seed in a floor file and `loadVesselSigningSeed` reads it without a human route (`vault-carriers.ts:240-242`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442`). ''Option A:'' keep a *named floor signer* owned by the node, protected at rest under an explicit mechanism; floor means no custody S key/FCK, while this narrowly scoped signer can support faceless Herm startup. Specify the protection, allowed signing/derivation domains, and restart behavior. ''Option B:'' put that signer behind an explicit boot custody door; public card/relay can stand before unlock, while operations requiring its signature wait/refuse until the door opens. Specify Herm boot and unattended container behavior. Neither option is selected here. The private seed is never F, never the current S key, and never the empty serving SK.
263:# ''Node archive row selector.'' The single `keyhive-archive` carrier is proven (`vault-carriers.ts:290-291`), but the path from each node provider/archive save to the selected face row remains unproved in the handbacks (round 1's YIN handback in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">>; round 4's YANG handback in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">>). Trace every node archive writer and restore reader and show an authenticated PersonaGroup selector before migration. Until then, the D target is a ''LEAD'' and bytes do not move.
264:# ''Tablet unattended sync.'' Public and ciphertext copy is F. Unattended D decrypt/transform needs a separate product ruling: route it to an authorized always-on node or define a narrowly named S principal, payload and boot door. No background worker receives an FCK by implication (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`).
265:# ''Guest-owned store.'' Prove whether the guest persists its own keys/rows. If yes, it is a separate vessel with its own D/F split, FCK trees, floor, and isolation red; if no, every private guest act remains under the host's admitted face session. Host keys never silently become guest keys (`stable-founding-road.mem:207`).

